    {
      "id": "oPWXVkNFM-peSqqCyGJ85",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2493.8750385697467,
      "y": -1474.2508897169541,
      "width": 2078.918701171875,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "7LOYqQ3GlYuGjbJKAB7bC",
      "index": "a1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2040577229,
      "version": 336,
      "versionNonce": 653827651,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784652148644,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**Papers (read, don't skim):**\n- TPU v4 — [arXiv:2304.01433](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01433). OCS, twisted torus, SparseCore. Source of truth for your topology question.\n- Jouppi et al., *In-Datacenter Performance Analysis of a TPU* (ISCA 2017). Why a systolic array.\n- FlashAttention / FlashAttention-2. Non-negotiable.\n- *Efficient Memory Management for LLM Serving with PagedAttention* (vLLM, SOSP '23).\n- Orca (OSDI '22) — iteration-level (continuous) batching. The scheduling idea everything else is built on.\n- DistServe / Splitwise — prefill/decode disaggregation.\n\n**Docs that replace books:**\n- JAX Pallas docs, *especially* \"Writing TPU kernels with Pallas → details.\"\n- `openxla.org/xprof` + the XProf Kernel Profiling guide.\n- [Ironwood performance optimizations](https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/ironwood-performance) — SparseCore collective offloading, remat policy selection, sharding-strategy-by-workload table.\n- [TPU v4 topology](https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v4) — twisted torus rules.\n- MLX \"Custom Extensions in C++ and Metal.\"\n\n**Books, ranked by marginal value to *this* goal:**\n1. **Programming Massively Parallel Processors, 4th ed.** — you have it. Ch. 5–6, then the FlashAttention-adjacent chapters. The single best book you own for this.\n2. **Designing Data-Intensive Applications** — you have it. Read it for the serving/distributed-systems interview, not for accelerators.\n3. **High Performance Python** — you have it. Profiling discipline.\n4. *Deep Learning with JAX* — you have it. Fine as a JAX on-ramp; it will not teach you Pallas.\n5. **A modern C++ book** — you have none, and you need one. *Effective Modern C++* or *C++ Concurrency in Action*. You cannot write an MLX Primitive or read XLA without this, and no CUDA book substitutes.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**Papers (read, don't skim):**\n- TPU v4 — [arXiv:2304.01433](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01433). OCS, twisted torus, SparseCore. Source of truth for your topology question.\n- Jouppi et al., *In-Datacenter Performance Analysis of a TPU* (ISCA 2017). Why a systolic array.\n- FlashAttention / FlashAttention-2. Non-negotiable.\n- *Efficient Memory Management for LLM Serving with PagedAttention* (vLLM, SOSP '23).\n- Orca (OSDI '22) — iteration-level (continuous) batching. The scheduling idea everything else is built on.\n- DistServe / Splitwise — prefill/decode disaggregation.\n\n**Docs that replace books:**\n- JAX Pallas docs, *especially* \"Writing TPU kernels with Pallas → details.\"\n- `openxla.org/xprof` + the XProf Kernel Profiling guide.\n- [Ironwood performance optimizations](https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/ironwood-performance) — SparseCore collective offloading, remat policy selection, sharding-strategy-by-workload table.\n- [TPU v4 topology](https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v4) — twisted torus rules.\n- MLX \"Custom Extensions in C++ and Metal.\"\n\n**Books, ranked by marginal value to *this* goal:**\n1. **Programming Massively Parallel Processors, 4th ed.** — you have it. Ch. 5–6, then the FlashAttention-adjacent chapters. The single best book you own for this.\n2. **Designing Data-Intensive Applications** — you have it. Read it for the serving/distributed-systems interview, not for accelerators.\n3. **High Performance Python** — you have it. Profiling discipline.\n4. *Deep Learning with JAX* — you have it. Fine as a JAX on-ramp; it will not teach you Pallas.\n5. **A modern C++ book** — you have none, and you need one. *Effective Modern C++* or *C++ Concurrency in Action*. You cannot write an MLX Primitive or read XLA without this, and no CUDA book substitutes.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
