    {
      "id": "pArNoTPDd__LgTfZKbPN4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 619.8298650528886,
      "y": 2615.8866361521596,
      "width": 578.7196655273438,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "Oz6Z8FmKiXG6vG7c4_PXr",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1608538605,
      "version": 1111,
      "versionNonce": 2079337623,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784660216703,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Frequeqntly asked questions\n- how do I import another file (OOP) is it like __init__.py?\n- what are fundamentals?\n    - data types\n        - str\n        - int\n        - float\n        - bool\n    - logic flow\n        - if/else\n        - try/except/finally/else\n    - error handling\n        - assert\n        - try/except/raise\n        - if __name__ == \"__main__\"\n\n- what are data structures and algorithims\n    - ... machine learning?\n    - ... deep learning?\n    - ... cuda? (NVIDIA GPU)\n    - ... XLA? (Google TPU)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Frequeqntly asked questions\n- how do I import another file (OOP) is it like __init__.py?\n- what are fundamentals?\n    - data types\n        - str\n        - int\n        - float\n        - bool\n    - logic flow\n        - if/else\n        - try/except/finally/else\n    - error handling\n        - assert\n        - try/except/raise\n        - if __name__ == \"__main__\"\n\n- what are data structures and algorithims\n    - ... machine learning?\n    - ... deep learning?\n    - ... cuda? (NVIDIA GPU)\n    - ... XLA? (Google TPU)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "aO"
    },
    {
      "id": "KfYfcbXOEBhEak6GghksU",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": -2270.5057945452118,
      "y": 2422.74653878178,
      "width": 2237.8385416666656,
      "height": 1057.4140625000005,
      "name": "GPU/TPU & Accelerator Computing — Senior-Level Best Practices",
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 95549468,
      "version": 267,
      "versionNonce": 814618455,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784659892119,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "index": "aP"
    },
    {
      "id": "27q-x-Zv5xi3_Bd_2AgIv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2246.5057945452118,
      "y": 2476.7465387817806,
      "width": 2038.0091145833344,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KfYfcbXOEBhEak6GghksU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1048907513,
      "version": 267,
      "versionNonce": 2006914167,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784659892119,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Memory hierarchy: SRAM/registers >> HBM in bandwidth and latency — the fastest kernel keeps data resident in on-chip memory (TPU VMEM, GPU shared mem/L2) and minimizes round-trips to HBM. This is\nwhy fused kernels (FlashAttention) beat naive op-by-op execution even at equal FLOP counts.\n- Host-device transfer overhead: any CPU<->accelerator copy (dataloading, `.item()`/`.cpu()` calls, per-step logging/asserts) is often the real bottleneck, not compute.\n  - Use pinned memory and async copy streams to overlap transfer with compute\n  - Batch/aggregate host-side syncs instead of forcing one every step\n- Mixed precision — bf16 vs fp16: bf16 keeps fp32's dynamic range with fp16's throughput, so it doesn't need loss scaling and is the safer default for large-model training; fp16 is faster on some older\nhardware paths but requires dynamic loss scaling to avoid gradient underflow.\n- fp8: the newest speed tier (NVIDIA H100+, TPU v5p/Trillium+) — needs per-tensor or per-block scaling and is typically reserved for the largest matmuls (attention QK^T, MLP projections) while master\nweights/optimizer state stay in higher precision.\n- Numerics discipline: keep a fp32 (or bf16) master copy of weights/optimizer state even when compute runs in a lower precision — accumulation error compounds over millions of steps and is invisible until a\nloss spike or divergence far into a run.\n- Data parallel (DP): replicate the full model per device, shard the batch — simplest mental model, but activation memory scales with per-device batch size and it needs an all-reduce of gradients every step.\nHits a wall once the model itself no longer fits on one device.\n- Tensor parallel (TP): shard individual layers' weight matrices across devices (Megatron-style column/row-parallel linear layers). Cuts per-device memory but synchronizes *inside* every forward/backward pass,\nso it needs high-bandwidth, low-latency interconnect (NVLink / TPU ICI) — generally kept within a node/pod.\n- Pipeline parallel (PP): shard the model by layer/stage across devices and stream micro-batches through. Communication is coarser (activations between adjacent stages only), which tolerates slower\ninterconnects across nodes, but introduces pipeline-bubble overhead — needs enough micro-batches to keep all stages busy.\n- Choosing a strategy: DP when the model fits per-device and you want throughput/simplicity; TP for within-node scaling when a single layer is too large; PP for scaling across nodes with slower links.\nProduction LLM training composes all three (\"3D parallelism\") plus ZeRO/FSDP-style parameter/optimizer-state sharding to remove redundant memory.\n- Profiling workflow: start from the timeline trace, not intuition — first classify the bottleneck as compute-bound (SM/MXU utilization high, kernels back-to-back), memory-bandwidth-bound, or communication-\nbound (visible gaps waiting on collectives).\n- What to actually look for in a trace:\n  - Idle gaps between kernels — usually host-side Python overhead or unfused ops\n  - Disproportionately long all-reduce/all-gather bars — comm-bound, check whether they overlap with compute\n  - Many small/short kernels dominating wall time — launch-overhead-bound, a fusion candidate\n  - Step-to-step timing variance — a signal of stragglers, thermal throttling, or resource contention\n- Practical tools: PyTorch Profiler + Chrome trace viewer/TensorBoard, NVIDIA Nsight Systems (timeline) and Nsight Compute (per-kernel detail) for CUDA, JAX/XLA's built-in profiler + XProf/TensorBoard\nfor TPU. Always profile a few warmed-up steps — the first includes compilation/warmup and skews the numbers.\n- RAII in ML systems: CUDA/C++ runtime code wraps raw resource handles (streams, device memory, cuBLAS/cuDNN handles) in RAII classes so destructors free them on scope exit or exception. Matters\nbecause accelerator memory leaks degrade a long-running training job silently rather than crashing immediately.\n- Move semantics: avoid unnecessary deep copies of large device-memory-backed buffers passed between C++ layers of a runtime — `std::move` and move constructors transfer ownership without copying,\nwhich is significant when a \"tensor\" wrapper object represents gigabytes on the device.\n- Templates in CUDA/ML runtimes: kernels are commonly templated on dtype (fp16/bf16/fp32) and tile/block size so the compiler emits specialized, branch-free code per instantiation instead of paying a\nruntime dispatch cost — this is why CUTLASS/cuDNN-style APIs look template-heavy.\n- C++ fundamentals to prioritize before CUDA/XLA: value vs. reference semantics, ownership (`unique_ptr`/`shared_ptr` vs. raw pointers), templates, and RAII. These four show up constantly in kernel\nwrapper code and runtime internals — more load-bearing early on than deep OOP class hierarchies.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Memory hierarchy: SRAM/registers >> HBM in bandwidth and latency — the fastest kernel keeps data resident in on-chip memory (TPU VMEM, GPU shared mem/L2) and minimizes round-trips to HBM. This is why fused kernels (FlashAttention) beat naive op-by-op execution even at equal FLOP counts.\n- Host-device transfer overhead: any CPU<->accelerator copy (dataloading, `.item()`/`.cpu()` calls, per-step logging/asserts) is often the real bottleneck, not compute.\n  - Use pinned memory and async copy streams to overlap transfer with compute\n  - Batch/aggregate host-side syncs instead of forcing one every step\n- Mixed precision — bf16 vs fp16: bf16 keeps fp32's dynamic range with fp16's throughput, so it doesn't need loss scaling and is the safer default for large-model training; fp16 is faster on some older hardware paths but requires dynamic loss scaling to avoid gradient underflow.\n- fp8: the newest speed tier (NVIDIA H100+, TPU v5p/Trillium+) — needs per-tensor or per-block scaling and is typically reserved for the largest matmuls (attention QK^T, MLP projections) while master weights/optimizer state stay in higher precision.\n- Numerics discipline: keep a fp32 (or bf16) master copy of weights/optimizer state even when compute runs in a lower precision — accumulation error compounds over millions of steps and is invisible until a loss spike or divergence far into a run.\n- Data parallel (DP): replicate the full model per device, shard the batch — simplest mental model, but activation memory scales with per-device batch size and it needs an all-reduce of gradients every step. Hits a wall once the model itself no longer fits on one device.\n- Tensor parallel (TP): shard individual layers' weight matrices across devices (Megatron-style column/row-parallel linear layers). Cuts per-device memory but synchronizes *inside* every forward/backward pass, so it needs high-bandwidth, low-latency interconnect (NVLink / TPU ICI) — generally kept within a node/pod.\n- Pipeline parallel (PP): shard the model by layer/stage across devices and stream micro-batches through. Communication is coarser (activations between adjacent stages only), which tolerates slower interconnects across nodes, but introduces pipeline-bubble overhead — needs enough micro-batches to keep all stages busy.\n- Choosing a strategy: DP when the model fits per-device and you want throughput/simplicity; TP for within-node scaling when a single layer is too large; PP for scaling across nodes with slower links. Production LLM training composes all three (\"3D parallelism\") plus ZeRO/FSDP-style parameter/optimizer-state sharding to remove redundant memory.\n- Profiling workflow: start from the timeline trace, not intuition — first classify the bottleneck as compute-bound (SM/MXU utilization high, kernels back-to-back), memory-bandwidth-bound, or communication-bound (visible gaps waiting on collectives).\n- What to actually look for in a trace:\n  - Idle gaps between kernels — usually host-side Python overhead or unfused ops\n  - Disproportionately long all-reduce/all-gather bars — comm-bound, check whether they overlap with compute\n  - Many small/short kernels dominating wall time — launch-overhead-bound, a fusion candidate\n  - Step-to-step timing variance — a signal of stragglers, thermal throttling, or resource contention\n- Practical tools: PyTorch Profiler + Chrome trace viewer/TensorBoard, NVIDIA Nsight Systems (timeline) and Nsight Compute (per-kernel detail) for CUDA, JAX/XLA's built-in profiler + XProf/TensorBoard for TPU. Always profile a few warmed-up steps — the first includes compilation/warmup and skews the numbers.\n- RAII in ML systems: CUDA/C++ runtime code wraps raw resource handles (streams, device memory, cuBLAS/cuDNN handles) in RAII classes so destructors free them on scope exit or exception. Matters because accelerator memory leaks degrade a long-running training job silently rather than crashing immediately.\n- Move semantics: avoid unnecessary deep copies of large device-memory-backed buffers passed between C++ layers of a runtime — `std::move` and move constructors transfer ownership without copying, which is significant when a \"tensor\" wrapper object represents gigabytes on the device.\n- Templates in CUDA/ML runtimes: kernels are commonly templated on dtype (fp16/bf16/fp32) and tile/block size so the compiler emits specialized, branch-free code per instantiation instead of paying a runtime dispatch cost — this is why CUTLASS/cuDNN-style APIs look template-heavy.\n- C++ fundamentals to prioritize before CUDA/XLA: value vs. reference semantics, ownership (`unique_ptr`/`shared_ptr` vs. raw pointers), templates, and RAII. These four show up constantly in kernel wrapper code and runtime internals — more load-bearing early on than deep OOP class hierarchies.",
